 - Consolidated **long, unneeded, or unused comments** into [Long_Comments/](/Long_Comments/) and replaced them with short custom markers or files to keep code clean, lean, and readable. > ~500k characters reduced and still ongoing.
 - **BBAI logging is now XML-tunable:** BBAI AI-decision logging can now be enabled/disabled and adjusted through **SAS defines**, rather than editing and recompiling the DLL as in base AdvCiv. This is deemed valuable **not only for modders, but also for users** who can now view or generate `BBAI.log` files for review. Note: `LoggingEnabled=1` in `CivilizationIV.ini` is still required for Civ4 to write. This allowed for example to surgically identify and fix the root cause of a city not producing its first settler until ~T90 at normal game speed, which is way too late (`bWarPlan` was too aggressively gating settler production; now it only blocks settler production after early expansion has reached enough cities (inefficient at that point, better switch strategy)). Note: BBAI logs can be very long; for broad raw-log diagnosis, we usually give to an external LLM/AI such as ChatGPT the log file, as it is as of now agentic-token-free analysis (also good to give us a different point of view/review if needed).
 - **Timestamped BBAI log files:** Optionally log each new game and loaded save to a **new UTC-timestamped file** (e.g., `BBAI_20260705T071718Z_new1.log` or `BBAI_20260705T071718Z_load2.log`) instead of expanding the existing `BBAI.log`, which was very tedious to clean up or identify/store/read/review/upload, and so repeated save-file tests no longer require restarting Civ4. Each log begins notably with the new/load time, currently active BBAI log levels, turn/year, map and game settings (including `landheavy` and `navalheavy` actual game information), player counts, and RNG states, avoiding mixed logs and the need to restart Civ4 between repeated save-file tests. See also [README.md (BBAI logging and head example)](/README.md#bbai-logging-and-head-example).
-- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6022+) **SAS game-summary log:** added a separate `SASGameSummary_*.log` for compact autoplay and AI-strength review. It records high-level run context such as economy, expansion, city and battle history, worked plots, diplomacy, unit composition, and game state, complementing BBAI logs by summarizing what happened before or alongside detailed AI-decision traces. Note: it currently contains spoilers: a spoiler-free implementation was considered, so the human/active player can give to an LLM or such their play summary and get LLM or such suggestions or feedback/analysis, but it would require an extensive rework, so currently this is not supported; therefore, SAS game-summary log should as of now not be treated as a spoiler-free player-advice export. Note 2: depending on `SAS_GAME_SUMMARY_TURN_INTERVAL` value, the last turn may not appear in the log: for example, an autoplay run of 200 turns ends at T200 and thus does not seem to show the T200 data, only up to T190, so autoplay at least 201 turns to show T200 for example if you have this issue. See also [README.md (DLL Logging)](/README.md#dll-logging).
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6022+) **SAS game-summary log:** added a separate `SASGameSummary_*.log` for compact autoplay and AI-strength review. It records high-level run context such as economy, expansion, city and battle history, worked plots, diplomacy, unit composition, game state, and in-game bonus appearances/disappearances, complementing BBAI logs by summarizing what happened before or alongside detailed AI-decision traces. Note: it currently contains spoilers: a spoiler-free implementation was considered, so the human/active player can give to an LLM or such their play summary and get LLM or such suggestions or feedback/analysis, but it would require an extensive rework, so currently this is not supported; therefore, SAS game-summary log should as of now not be treated as a spoiler-free player-advice export. Note 2: depending on `SAS_GAME_SUMMARY_TURN_INTERVAL` value, the last turn may not appear in the log: for example, an autoplay run of 200 turns ends at T200 and thus does not seem to show the T200 data, only up to T190, so autoplay at least 201 turns to show T200 for example if you have this issue. See also [README.md (DLL Logging)](/README.md#dll-logging).
 - **BBAI worker log split:** added separate worker and Work Boat / `UNITAI_WORKER_SEA` BBAI log-level defines so ordinary `SAS_BBAI_UNIT_LOG_LEVEL` can focus on non-worker unit behavior such as scrapping, army movement, and combat-unit decisions. Worker and worker-sea logs had become noisy enough to hide those unit decisions, and Work Boats use distinct logic from land workers, so they now have their own category as well.
 - **BBAI Settler log split:** added `SAS_BBAI_SETTLER_LOG_LEVEL`, separated from UNIT so Settlers and other units can be inspected independently. This notably covers city-site decisions, Settler movement, scouting, returns, founding, (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6027+) parking reasons, danger context, and city escort pools; FOUND as of now separately controls detailed city-site scoring calculations. This notably lets UNIT investigate other unit behavior without enabling the multi-megabyte first-city Settler traces needed for example for the Berlin, Karakorum, and Cuzco regression tests.
 - **Culture-victory BBAI diagnostics:** added a new dedicated `SAS_BBAI_CULTURE_LOG_LEVEL` notably for diagnosing premature, uneven, or wasteful AI culture investment without enabling broad player/city logs. It records for example culture-stage reasons, current/projected Legendary-city countdowns and rival ranking, competing victory progress, actual culture-slider decisions, per-city weights and production, and culture contributed by production processes and assigned specialists.
@@ -348,7 +348,7 @@ Note 2: Master–vassal(s) changes are intended to strengthen overall AI play by
 - **Compare the best useful work across all cities:** workers now compare each city's strongest reachable job by its complete Food/Production/Commerce/resource/terrain evaluation, with only a modest travel cost, instead of favoring cities mainly by population or staying near weaker local work. Cities that lack enough completed or already-assigned improvements for their population receive proportionally more help; counting assigned workers discourages crowding one city, while reserving one worker per plot prevents several workers from targeting the same tile. In BBAI testing in save file 442, this redirected workers from low-value unused Tundra Workshops toward valuable Grass/Plains Hill Mines in underdeveloped cities. See also [KI#39](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#39---seemingly-fixed-orand-enhanced-make-ai-workers-move-sooner-to-city-b-or-city-c-or-such-and-vice-versa-if-current-city-is-already-improved-enough-and-dont-need-to-be-over-improved-while-other-cities-are-not-improved-enough-and-would-much-rather-need-it) and [KI#41](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#41---seemingly-fixed-beyond-tremendously-improved-ai-worker-mobility-flexibility-and-reliability-now-favouring-minimal-big-city-improvement-come-back-to-it-later-but-dont-delay-improving-smaller-ones-quick-moving-to-smaller-ones-and-spending-longer-to-improve-smaller-ones-as-they-grow-fast-but-as-well-as-being-braver-in-our-own-cultural-borders-orand-moving-to-other-cities-needing-improvements-rather-than-being-parked-in-current-city-if-im-not-mistaken-but-and-such-other-changes-to-increase-ai-efficiency-reliably-and-other-changes-if-any-thanks-to-chatgpt-5-and-me-too-if-i-may-say-but).
 - **Worker mobility / overimprovement refinement:** workers still rotate away from sufficiently improved small cities, but medium and large cities are less likely to leave useful BFC plots unimproved. Workers also avoid ordinary replacement churn (e.g. **Mine ↔ Windmill**, **Cottage ↔ Farm**) while valid unimproved BFC work remains, except for bonus fixes and irrigation-chain Farms. The small-city rotation shortcut does not make a worker leave when it is already standing on this same city's useful BFC plot and that plot is the city's best current job.
 - **Finish valuable followup builds after feature removal:** a worker can complete standalone Forest/Jungle/Fallout removal (including receiving Forest chop production sooner, separated from e.g. a 2 in 1 mine that would deliver hammer on feature remove later plus would be less flexible), then continue directly into the worthwhile Mine, Farm, Cottage, resource improvement, etc. selected for that plot. This prevents workers from chopping or clearing a strong tile, walking away, and paying another trip later (which is especially inefficient if this costs movement/delay again); weak ordinary follow-ups are still reconsidered instead of being forced. Queued follow-ups now wait for the worker's next turn when removal consumes all movement rather than being silently discarded. See also [KI#176](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#176---fixed-base-advcivk-mod-mission-queue-bug-exposed-by-advciv-sas-worker-follow-up-improvements-stale-ai-worker-build-target-could-execute-on-the-wrong-plot).
-- **Retreat logic:** fix excessive retreat → "parking in city" edge cases with a wake-from-retreat and saner thresholds. See [KI#50](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#50---tremendously-improvedfixedenhanced-excessive-ai-worker-retreat-logic-causing-worker-parking-in-cities-in-rare-cases-now-added-a-wake-from-retreat-and-other-changes-if-any-other-change-i-mean).
+- **Retreat logic:** fix excessive retreat → "parking in city" edge cases with a wake-from-retreat and saner thresholds. See [KI#50](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#50---tremendously-improvedfixedenhanced-excessive-ai-worker-retreat-logic-causing-worker-parking-in-cities-in-rare-cases-now-added-a-wake-from-retreat-and-other-changes-if-any-other-change). (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6030+) Update: while this was meant initially to help reduce excessive Worker parking or inactivity, this may have also caused excessive or avoidable Worker deaths, so danger now handling also treats current plot danger as real even inside owned culture, while still ignoring broad inside-border threat-radius noise that caused too much retreating; in save-file-450 BBAI testing, this prevented a Worker from continuing a build on a currently dangerous plot and reduced comparable t200 Worker deaths from 40 to 17.
 - **Worker irrigation-chain hunting:** updated SAS AI worker logic so it now **starts from actual dry-farm / low-food BFC targets** and **hunts short owned irrigation-chain steps**, including first links **outside the city BFC**. This fixed Chaco Canyon and Harappan-style cases where a nearby (2-3 plots away from BFC) water source existed but BFC-only worker logic missed the first link. Workers now retain only the strongest available connector toward each target instead of treating several alternative routes to the same Farm as separate urgent jobs, leaving other workers free to pursue valuable improvements elsewhere.
 
